00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.020
And I'm going to
start with an anecdote

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.240
about the opportunities
and challenges of cloud

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.640
and what this means for
financial governance.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.620
So let's suspend
disbelief and pretend

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.840
we all work for Acme Company.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.640
Here is a cartoonish
view of what our resource

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.320
consumption might look like.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.360
We're a growing business,
so our costs are

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.920
trending up and to the right.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.130
And we have these
seasonal fluctuations

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:24.060
as our business demands change
and contrast over the course

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.200
of a year or a month.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.750
Now, back when we ran
our own data center,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.420
we had to procure
hardware ahead of time.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.680
We had to provision
for the peak.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.860
We had to forecast our
demand ahead of time.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.902
And that's represented by
this dotted green line.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.510
And we don't have a lot of
contrast in terms of color

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.187
here, but I'll talk
through it anyway.

00:03:49.187 --> 00:03:50.770
Sometimes we
under-provisioned, right?

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.075
And we can't serve all
our customer demand.

00:03:53.075 --> 00:03:54.450
You can see our
dotted green line

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.130
is underneath our
blue demand line.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:00.520
And then we have a degraded
end consumer experience--

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.700
slow page load times,
unresponsive applications,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.660
all bad news.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.240
Maybe we're even able to
keep our core workloads up,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.600
but we have to restrict our
developers discretionary use.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.300
And that slows our time
to market, also bad news.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:20.829
Alternatively, other
times we over-provision,

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:23.630
and now we have all of this
wasted resources that are just

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.480
sitting idle because
we over-provisioned,

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.170
and we have too many servers.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.840
So we have a bunch of wasted
costs, still bad news.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.770
Fast forward to today, we've
moved on to public cloud,

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.797
and now we're able to capture
all these savings because we

00:04:44.797 --> 00:04:46.380
don't have to provision
ahead of time,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.870
we don't have the
bridge in for the peak,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.510
we have no more long
procurement lead times,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.310
and we have no more
wasted capacity like this.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.160
So this is the benefit
of cloud, right?

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.720
We just pay for what we need.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.940
And now when we have that
unexpected spike in demand,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.950
we can scale up because of
the elasticity of cloud.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.680
So that shaded yellow
area represents

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.480
something we wouldn't have
been able to do before

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:09.688
if we hadn't procured for it.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.640
But the flexibility
that we get with Cloud

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.750
comes with a new challenge,
which is all of a sudden

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.730
we can serve demand
that maybe wasn't

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:25.880
as important for our business.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.610
Back with our data center,
our costs were literally

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.060
bounded by the physical
servers we bought and put there

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.260
and the software
licenses we run on them.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.615
But now we can generate costs
from anything that happens.

00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:39.990
One of my engineering
teams could

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.240
spin up a large-scale
testing environment

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.250
and forget to turn it back off.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.830
Or a business analyst
on my team might

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.400
write a bad query that performs
a join on a really, really

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.270
big data set and incurs a
commensurately large BigQuery

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.020
build.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.270
Raise your hand if you've
seen one of these two things

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.360
or something of the like?

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.360
Yeah, we've got some hands up.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.070
I tend to write queries
brute force trial and error,

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.540
so I have personally had
experience with the second one.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.170
So that brings us to the
point of the session today.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:17.260
We're going to talk about how to
get back in the driver's seat.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.360
Financial governance is
all about making a plan

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.960
and sticking to it.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.490
It's about avoiding
surprises on your bill.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:27.130
It's about giving you the
flexibility to scale to meet

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.470
the demands of your business
where you want to, where it

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.840
adds value, and the control
to restrict resources where it

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:33.340
doesn't.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.110
So I'm going to read this quote.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.360
It comes from a Gartner study.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.440
I think it sums up the
situation pretty well.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.513
"90% the respondents--"
to their survey--

00:06:48.513 --> 00:06:50.180
"agree that public
cloud will save money

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.530
for their organizations.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.040
However, few companies
have implemented

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.710
financial management
processes for public cloud.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.800
Therefore, few have
any idea whether they

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.300
are achieving their goals."

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.040
Think about that.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.040
It is pretty clear that
there is a massive disconnect

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.880
between what many of us know
is important for our business

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.100
and our ability to actually
go achieve those goals.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.660
So, let's get back
in the driver's seat.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.080
We're going to do that in three
kind of simple steps today.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.720
We need to plan to make sure
we know what our costs are

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.030
expected to be moving forwards.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.940
We're going to monitor our
costs against that plan,

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.020
triage alerts, notify people who
might want to go take action.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.780
And then we're going to
actually go control our costs,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.870
put guardrails in place
to protect ourselves

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:45.870
from unexpected expense.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.020
First up is planning.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:52.310
It's the essential first step.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.810
And the way that
we're going to do this

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.840
varies a lot depending on what
our organization looks like,

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.108
what our processes are.

00:08:02.108 --> 00:08:03.900
So I'm not going to go
into too much detail

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.820
on this particular step.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.310
But I am going to go over
some of the tools that

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.580
make this easier on GCP.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.590
So here I have a screenshot
from our Billing Reports tool.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.760
You can find this in
the Billing console.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.650
And this is a fairly
simple dashboarding UI

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:24.480
that allows you to view your
historical cost trends on GCP.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.010
This is actually an example
from a large internal customer.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.080
And you can see some
simple things here.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.110
Our costs are overall growing.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.945
And then about halfway
through the chart,

00:08:34.945 --> 00:08:36.570
you'll see that
there's this inflection

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.299
point where the chart--

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:40.780
the cost started
to grow a lot more.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.780
And this actually happened
because they launched

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:46.870
a new feature that
went viral with users,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.030
and all of a sudden,
their costs started

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.090
to grow with that new feature.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.610
And we can look at that new
trend, forecast it forwards,

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:57.070
and see if business
continues as usual, what

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.604
are my costs going to look like
over the next month, quarter,

00:09:01.604 --> 00:09:02.104
year?

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.990
If I wanted to go into a
little bit more detail,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.450
I could use the Billing Export.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.670
We'll pipe your
data into BigQuery

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.250
with a lot more granularity
so you can look at this

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.550
and slice it by region,
or label, or even

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.020
things like machine
shape, get down

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:20.910
to a really fine level
of detail if it's

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.890
important for my
planning processes.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.808
And once I've accounted
for my organic growth,

00:09:28.808 --> 00:09:30.600
there is some things
that Google's just not

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.190
going to know about.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:34.950
Like let's say I'm going to
launch a new application,

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:39.480
and I expect it to have 100
daily actives with x footprint

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.810
of storage and compute needs,
I can make that capacity plan,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.780
but Google has no way of
knowing about it ahead of time.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.450
Or maybe I'm going to
launch into Europe and Asia

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.220
for the first time,
and I need to spin up

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.970
new instances of my services
in those new regions.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.510
Once I have that capacity
plan, the Google Cloud Platform

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.130
pricing calculator will help me
turn that into a financial cost

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:03.883
analysis that I
can then build into

00:10:03.883 --> 00:10:05.050
my forward-looking forecast.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.230
But stepping back from
tooling for a sec,

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:14.190
regardless of whether you use
GCP tooling, or spreadsheets,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.380
or some sort of
third-party software,

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:19.170
just remember to make
cloud financial governance

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.258
part of your financial
planning process,

00:10:21.258 --> 00:10:22.800
and whatever your
regular cadence is,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.660
monthly, quarterly,
annual, et cetera.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:34.100
So, now that we have a
well-reasoned financial plan,

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.130
let's actually get to
the interesting part.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.880
We need to put in place
some monitoring so

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.470
that we know when we're getting
off track from that plan.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:45.192
So at this point, I'm
tired of looking at slides.

00:10:45.192 --> 00:10:46.900
I hope you're tired
of looking at slides.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.420
Let's actually hop into
the console and start

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.170
doing some demos.

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:55.780
Awesome.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.400
So this is the
Budgets and Alerts

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.610
UI in the Billing Console.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:04.510
This is the core toolkit
that GCP provides you

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.612
for monitoring your spend.

00:11:06.612 --> 00:11:08.530
And how it works is this.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:10.720
You'll create a budget
once for the scope

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.620
of spend that you care about.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.770
Let's say, it's an
application or a project.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:18.370
And then Google will, behind the
scenes, monitor costs for you

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:20.470
against that budget
into perpetuity

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.140
and let you know if you're
getting off track from what

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.880
you think is reasonable.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:26.960
So for the purposes
of this demo,

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.960
let's say that at
Acme Company, we're

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.720
launching a new application,
and we're calling it

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:35.150
the Amazing Application.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.440
We think we know how many
users are going to be on it.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.635
We think we know that it's going
to cost about $1,000 per month.

00:11:40.635 --> 00:11:42.510
So now we'll create a
budget to monitor that.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:48.340
I'll name it the Amazing
Application Budget.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:53.778
Excellent.

00:11:53.778 --> 00:11:55.570
I think I've been frozen
a little too long.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.100
OK, there we go.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:11.450
I'm going to set it to monitor
Phil's Amazing Application

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:14.148
because I just want it to look
after this one application.

00:12:14.148 --> 00:12:16.190
I don't want to dilute it
with the spend from all

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.270
of the other projects
that I have running.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:23.190
And again, I'm going to give it
a budget of $1,000 per month.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.590
So after I figure out and
lock in how much I think

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:27.630
I'm going to spend
for this application,

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.250
then I get to go set my
alert threshold rules.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.310
And this defines the
notification behavior

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:33.970
that I want Google to follow.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.960
By default, we're
going to alert you

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.970
when you cross 50%, 90%, and
100% of that budget amount.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.440
So when the amazing
application spends $500,

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:47.900
we're going to send you
your first notification.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.460
But in this case, let's say
I don't actually want that.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.010
I want it to be a little more
like my cell phone data usage

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:54.630
plan, so I just want
to know when I'm

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.150
about to go over the budget.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:02.100
So I'll just keep 90% and 100%.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:05.910
And I'm actually going to add
one more alert notification.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.930
And instead of triggering
on actual spend,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.680
I'm going to trigger
on forecasted spend.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:14.460
And I'm going to
set that to 110%.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.070
So what that means
is if I'm forecasted

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.050
to go above my
budget amount, even

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:20.880
if it's early in the
month, then I'm going

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.290
to get an alert notification.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.030
So in this case,
let's say I'm spending

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.975
way more than expected,
pretty early on

00:13:25.975 --> 00:13:28.350
we're going to let you know
that you're off track to miss

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:29.945
your budget, even
if it's only, say,

00:13:29.945 --> 00:13:31.070
the third day of the month.

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:37.640
Now I hit Save, I lock
that in as a budget,

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.510
and you can see that here we
now have the Amazing Application

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:41.410
Budget.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:42.290
It's configured.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:46.340
We've spent about $364 of our
$1,000 budget for the month,

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:48.070
so we're maybe roughly on track.

00:13:50.687 --> 00:13:53.270
I don't want to wait for us to
get really close to the budget,

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:55.120
so I'm actually just going to
cut over back to the slides

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.640
and show you what an alert
notification would look like.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.990
So let's imagine the Amazing
Application goes viral,

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.840
and we have way more
users than expected.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:11.350
All of a sudden, I get this
alert notification in my inbox.

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:15.670
It says, you are forecasted
to spend more than 110%

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:16.600
of your budget.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.392
In this case, it was
for the month of March

00:14:18.392 --> 00:14:20.750
because that was last month.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:22.330
So now I have this
information, and I

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:23.540
can go do something with it.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.320
I can have a conversation
with my engineering team.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.720
Maybe our users
are adopting faster

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.880
than expected-- good news.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:33.022
I probably don't want to
take any major action.

00:14:33.022 --> 00:14:34.480
Maybe I'll just
increase the budget

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:37.710
so I don't get this
notification in the future.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.860
But maybe our user growth
is not as fast as expected,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.880
and then I want to take
some other sort of action,

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.110
which brings us to control.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.413
So far, all we've talked about
is monitoring and planning.

00:14:52.413 --> 00:14:54.080
But we actually want
to take some action

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:55.867
to make sure that our
costs don't go over

00:14:55.867 --> 00:14:56.950
budget in the first place.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:05.300
And the first thing we can do is
kind of what I just described.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.700
We get an alert and
signal, and we have

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.110
to decide what to do with it.

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:11.490
We triage that with
the engineering team,

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.000
we decide maybe we have some
over provisioned virtual

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.100
machines, and we can
go downsize those

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.600
so that we save some money.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.237
Maybe our storage is growing
faster than expected,

00:15:22.237 --> 00:15:24.070
and we want to migrate
some of those objects

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:27.150
over to cold line storage.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.310
We have some other tools
at our disposal as well.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:32.340
Quota is a tool that allows
us to throttle our resource

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.490
consumption so that
we actually rate limit

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.980
how much our spending can
increase moving forwards.

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.590
And I'll let Aurelie talk more
about that a little bit later

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.740
in the presentation.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:46.770
Kind of the last biggest
hammer in our arsenal

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:50.220
is to actually remove
billing from a project.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:53.975
So we can take a
project, disable billing.

00:15:53.975 --> 00:15:55.350
And what that will
effectively do

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.920
is stop all costs
from being accrued,

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.390
but it will also immediately
disable and delete

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.940
all resources and workloads that
are running in that project.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.220
So it's kind of an
irrevocable decision.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:09.720
And it might be fitting in a
very discretionary dev project

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:11.700
where the consequences
of deleting stuff

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.430
are not a big deal,
and where people

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:16.920
might be making more
careless mistakes,

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.540
and where it's just like
an easy decision to make,

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.160
so I'll just get
it out of the way.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.160
But for something like a
production environment,

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.720
that could be a very
costly decision.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.845
We probably wouldn't
recommend that type of action.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.160
The other thing we can
do is put guardrails

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:36.540
in place proactively.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:39.353
So never let spending get
out of control to begin with.

00:16:39.353 --> 00:16:41.520
And there's two levers that
we have at our disposal.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.860
One is resource restriction.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.750
Everything I just said
on the previous slide,

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:48.540
we can try to set that up
to happen automatically

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:52.650
before anything ever goes wrong.

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:54.520
For more production-like
environments,

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:56.770
this is where we think about
permission restriction is

00:16:56.770 --> 00:16:58.830
a better option because
we don't actually

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.963
want to degrade an
end-consumer experience,

00:17:00.963 --> 00:17:03.630
but we do want to make sure that
only a few authorized users are

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.335
making changes.

00:17:07.375 --> 00:17:08.750
For the rest of
the presentation,

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:09.829
though, we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:12.512
about resource restriction
in that dev test environment,

00:17:12.512 --> 00:17:14.720
and talk about how to put
automated controls in place

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:15.810
to do this on your behalf.

00:17:18.883 --> 00:17:20.300
So the first way
we can do this is

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.700
with a last feature of
budgets that I glossed over

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:27.260
in my previous demo called
Programmatic Notifications.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:29.990
In addition to
sending email alerts,

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:33.560
we can also configure budgets to
send programmatic notifications

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.240
to a Pub/Sub channel,
so it will constantly,

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.110
every 20 minutes or so,
ping the budget status

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.420
to this Pub/Sub channel.

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.510
And we can listen to this
Pub/Sub with a Cloud Function

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:48.280
and automate any kind
of logic we want.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:52.210
So one example would be, let's
say that my dev project hits

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.610
my $1,000 monthly budget.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:56.830
I actually want to go and
stop all virtual machines

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:57.550
from running.

00:17:57.550 --> 00:17:59.100
That was the
discretionary budget,

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.330
we've used it up, let's
stop spending money.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:03.430
And then if we need an
override, come talk to me,

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:05.602
we'll re-enable it, or
it just starts back up

00:18:05.602 --> 00:18:07.060
at the beginning
of the next month.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:13.120
Who wants to see me actually
try to do this right now?

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.150
I got some hands, yes.

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:19.140
That's the mildly enthusiastic
response I was hoping for.

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.092
Awesome.

00:18:25.707 --> 00:18:27.540
So I'm going to pop
into a different billing

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:32.630
account, my Budget Demo account,
and create another budget.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:37.940
I'm going to this one
my next19-budget-demo,

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:40.982
and give it $1,000 budget.

00:18:40.982 --> 00:18:43.190
I don't care so much about
the alert email thresholds

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:44.110
this time.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:46.600
But what I do want to
do that's different

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.372
is connect a Pub/Sub topic.

00:18:48.372 --> 00:18:50.830
And you can do this down at
the bottom of the Create Budget

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:51.330
screen.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:01.370
I'm going to set it to monitor--

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:07.358
or to post to the
next19-budget-demo topic,

00:19:07.358 --> 00:19:08.150
and I'll save that.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:13.860
So now I have my
NEXT 19 Budget Demo.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.990
And it's going to send
these budget status alerts

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.730
to this Pub/Sub
channel, right here,

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:21.130
the next19-budget-demo
Pub/Sub channel.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:27.620
You'll notice that I have
one thing subscribing

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:29.060
to this Pub/Sub
channel right now.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:32.974
And that's a Cloud Function
called gcf budget demo compute

00:19:32.974 --> 00:19:34.520
2 next19 budget demo.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:39.350
It's a long name, but I
wanted it to be descriptive.

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:44.120
And this is that Cloud Function
that's listening to it.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.120
You can find all of this
code in example demos

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:50.720
in our technical documentation,
so don't feel like you

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.123
need to copy all this down.

00:19:52.123 --> 00:19:53.540
You can definitely
find it online,

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.340
but I'm going to walk
you through what it does.

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:58.850
So the first thing
is I'm going to parse

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:02.910
the Pub/Sub payload to get
the relevant information.

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:07.870
Then, if I have not yet passed
the 100% budget threshold,

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.170
so if I'm still under
my budget amount,

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:13.400
I'm actually not
going to do anything.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.170
As soon as I pass that
threshold, though,

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:17.690
that's when I start
to take action.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:22.570
I'm going to authenticate
with the Google Cloud APIs.

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.170
I'm going to go get the
list of running instances

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:26.610
from GCE in that project.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.433
I'm going to stop
the instances that

00:20:32.433 --> 00:20:33.600
are running in that project.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.590
And then because my
team really hates email,

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.220
and they really like
Slack, I'm going

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.470
to go post an update into
their Slack channel and say,

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:47.000
hey, I just shut down all
your VMs, just so you know.

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:54.640
Now, I also don't
want to wait around

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:57.560
for the rest of the month
to pass my $1,000 budget,

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.400
so we're going to fake
it just for the demo.

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:02.950
And I'm actually just going to
post the same Pub/Sub payload

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:05.800
that our budget service would
send so that we can fast

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:08.910
forward a little bit here.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:11.570
Let's say I pass
that 90% threshold.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:13.880
I've spent $900.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:18.360
When I send this message,
nothing should happen.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:22.600
And if we go look at
our GCE page, refresh,

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:24.460
all the instances
are still running,

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.264
nothing's really happened.

00:21:27.264 --> 00:21:31.547
But then all of a sudden, we
cross this 100% threshold.

00:21:31.547 --> 00:21:33.130
And when I hit this
button, you should

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:34.320
see a little more fireworks.

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:40.680
Let's go pop over,
check our Slack channel.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.900
You can see, hey, we
have a notification.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.138
The NEXT 19 Budget Demo has
exceeded 100% of the threshold.

00:21:47.138 --> 00:21:48.930
We've stopped four
running virtual machines

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:50.010
in the projects.

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:53.331
Sounds good, let's
see if it happened.

00:21:53.331 --> 00:21:55.250
And now you can see
the same project,

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:56.750
we've terminated all of the VMs.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.730
So at this point,
if virtual machines

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:00.930
are a big portion
of our project cost,

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:03.140
we've pretty effectively
stopped a lot of the costs

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:05.300
from accruing in that
dev project, which

00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:06.650
is exactly what we wanted.

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:09.410
And this all happened behind
the scenes, automated.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:12.618
Once I set it up, this'll
just happen any month.

00:22:12.618 --> 00:22:14.660
If we could just cut back
over to the slides now.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:21.920
So that's programmatic
notifications.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:25.420
And again, this is not
necessarily the one action

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:26.290
you want to take.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:28.120
It's more of a
flexible framework

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.670
that you can use to implement
whatever logic makes sense

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:33.310
for you, for your business,
for your consumers

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.185
within your organization.

00:22:35.185 --> 00:22:36.560
And now to talk
a little bit more

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:38.018
about some of the
other methods you

00:22:38.018 --> 00:22:40.430
can use to control your
costs actively on GCP,

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:42.875
I'm going to hand
it over to Aurelie.

00:22:42.875 --> 00:22:45.090
AURELIE FONTENY: Thank you.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:46.550
Thank you, Phillip.

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:47.390
Can you hear me?

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:47.890
Yeah.

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:54.400
So we've talked about
various form of controls.

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:57.070
And one of them is Quota.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:01.530
So if you think about Quota,
you can think about a cell phone

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:02.700
data plan.

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:06.330
And here, your quota would
be the data plan limit

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:08.360
that you set.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:13.490
So you can set Quota on
a select set of services,

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:18.960
either at runtime, or you can
do it at project creation time.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:21.950
So, for example, say you're
starting out with BigQuery,

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:28.890
and you expect a cost of about
$100 per day on BigQuery.

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:34.610
So now you know that if
your query usage reaches

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.640
like a cost of around
$1,000 per day,

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.370
then something
weird is going on.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:44.090
So you can set a quota
to provide guardrails

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:47.350
so it doesn't happen.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:51.000
And once you've set a quota, you
can also create a quota alert

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:55.950
to notify you just before
you hit that quota limit

00:23:55.950 --> 00:24:00.750
so that you can set back
the quota quickly in case

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:05.220
the use is actually legitimate.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:12.620
So we're going to go
ahead and also do a demo.

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:15.685
So let me go ahead and--

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:21.373
great.

00:24:21.373 --> 00:24:22.790
So here, we're
going to illustrate

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.110
what we've just talked about.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:31.940
So as you can see here, I'm
in a cloud console for Quota,

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:36.210
and I've selected
the service BigQuery.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:39.510
And we have a
query usage per day

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:43.720
that's unlimited by
default. Now what

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:53.120
I'm going to do is I'm
going to go ahead and set it

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:55.010
to one terabyte.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:03.000
So I'm getting a
message to confirm

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:07.050
that I'm OK to reduce the
quota limit by more than 10%.

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:08.550
So I'm going to confirm that.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:21.210
And as long as the quota that
is being reduced to the limit

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:22.792
that's--

00:25:22.792 --> 00:25:24.750
compared to the limit
that's set by the service

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:26.522
producer-- in this
case, it's BigQuery--

00:25:26.522 --> 00:25:27.480
should be pretty quick.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:32.220
So you can see here that it's
been updated to one terabyte.

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:35.520
If we look at our UI, you can
see that it's actually already

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.490
been updated.

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:39.500
In reality, it
takes a few minutes

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.180
to update our servers globally.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:46.460
So I have a cookie of this
project where I've already

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:48.800
set the limit to one terabyte.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.430
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:55.250
to show you that
in fact, I won't

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:59.360
be able to run a large query
that's over one terabyte.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:04.620
So here I have a large query,
and I'm going to click Run.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:08.170
OK, yeah, I have probably
set the result to be cached.

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:10.080
So now I'm going
to run it again,

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:13.590
but it's not cached this time.

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:16.910
So as you can see,
I get the message

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:21.160
that the quota has been
exceeded for query usage today.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:28.350
Now, I'm going to go ahead
and run another query.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:33.900
And this time it's a small
query that's about 100 meg.

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:38.020
So let's just make sure that
I'm not using cache results,

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:39.420
and we are going to run it.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:47.350
And this time, because
the query is pretty small,

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:52.810
it's under one terabyte, it's
actually running successfully.

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:55.410
So what we've done is
we've just demonstrated

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:59.760
that you can send
guardrails with Quota

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.190
on the query usage per
day that you can use.

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:09.130
Now let's go back to the slides.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:12.900
So we've shown you how you can
set Quota to set guardrails

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:14.580
on one project.

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:19.410
We've shown you how we can set
a budget on one project also

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:20.970
using the UI.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:25.740
Now what if you had tens
of projects where you need

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:29.370
to set quota and budget alerts?

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:33.940
In that case, going to the
UI is no longer an option.

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:37.150
So this is where you can
use APIs to programmatically

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:42.420
and grammatically manage
budgets and quotas.

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:48.220
So we have two APIs
here that are relevant.

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:54.600
The first one is the budget API,
and it's coming to Alpha soon.

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.810
And it will allow you to
create and manage budgets

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:04.670
with the same nobs that you're
used to today in the UI.

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:08.210
The second one here is
the Service Usage API.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:11.440
It's already in
Alpha today, and it

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:16.540
allows you to
modify Quota as long

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.910
as it is below the
default that is

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:22.850
set by the service producers.

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:26.450
So then what you can do is you
can use your own tooling, so

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:30.590
your own scripts, or use
deployment management tool

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:38.480
to go ahead and create budgets
and Quota guardrails at project

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:39.190
creation time.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:47.290
So let's go ahead,
and we're going

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:54.000
to do a demo now
to just illustrate

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.840
what we just talked about.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:58.950
And we are going to do it
using a Service Usage API.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.110
And what we're going to
do is every time we create

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:08.340
a new project, we're going to
put basic guardrails in place

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:11.920
to control our
BigQuery analysis cost.

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:15.540
So we will create
a default policy

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:20.160
to limit the BigQuery analysis
to $1,000 per day, which

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:24.390
equates to 200 terabytes
in this case in terms

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:26.650
of query usage per day.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:30.420
So as you can see here, we
are first creating a project.

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:39.020
And then we are granting an
existing service account--

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:39.820
is it here?

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:40.320
Yeah.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.290
We are granting an existing
service account called

00:29:43.290 --> 00:29:47.820
su-quota, the service
usage admin role,

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:53.450
so it can actually change the
quota on the target project.

00:29:53.450 --> 00:29:57.950
And then the last step is to set
the BigQuery daily quota to 200

00:29:57.950 --> 00:29:59.760
terabytes instead of unlimited.

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:02.210
So you can see here
that I'm setting

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:04.550
the quota to 200 terabytes.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:07.760
And this is the quota that
we are actually setting.

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.850
So now I'm going to cut over
to the demo, which is basically

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:14.795
reusing the script.

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:27.300
So you can see I'm
creating a project,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:32.160
I'm granting a service account,
the service usage admin role.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:37.310
I am setting the BigQuery
daily quota to 100 terabytes.

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:45.190
And so I'm going to create
a project called CST Dev01.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:50.230
And right now, it's creating it.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:31:00.240
And now, I'm going to grant
the role to the service usage--

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:02.610
the service usage admin role.

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:04.160
You can see that.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:10.550
And then I'm going to set
the quota to 200 terabytes.

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:15.550
So now let's go
ahead and take a look

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:17.005
at our newly created project.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.250
So this is the project
we've just created.

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:39.820
And here we are looking at
the Cloud Console for Quota.

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:45.760
And if we are looking at
the query usage per day,

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:55.130
it's been set to 200 terabytes--
oh no, 20 terabytes actually.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:56.360
I guess I didn't change it.

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:57.786
It's 20 terabytes.

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:12.290
So that-- let me see.

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:13.870
Whoops.

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:18.320
Yeah, you can see actually
it was 20 terabytes, not 200.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:24.480
Anyway, so what
I've just shown you

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:32.400
is how to programmatically set
the quota at project creation.

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:37.000
And now this basically
concludes the presentation.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:40.590
So I'm going to go and recap
what we've talked about.

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:43.530
So we've talked about what
financial governance is

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.150
and why it's important.

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:49.380
We've talked about how do to
establish financial governance

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.990
using cost management tools.

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:55.020
And in particular, those
cost management tools

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:59.580
are budget, and the
associated alert notification

00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:02.570
for monitoring cost.

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:04.640
And for reactive
control, you can

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:09.630
use a service-specific
cloud consoles and APIs.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:12.520
And to proactively
control your costs,

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:15.690
you can use programmatic
budget notifications

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:18.780
in conjunction with
the Cloud Functions.

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:23.130
And you can also use Quota
to put in some guardrails.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:30.380
